Describe the bug
Description:
The application exposes a vulnerable account deletion endpoint:

Endpoint: https://ner-project.aixblock.io/api/users/{user_id}
Method: DELETE
This endpoint allows any authenticated user to delete any other user’s account simply by modifying the user_id in the URL. The backend fails to validate whether the requestor is the actual owner of the account being deleted.

The request results in a 201 Created response and the target account is fully and permanently deleted. There are no confirmation steps or ownership validation mechanisms in place.

To Reproduce
1.Create two accounts (Account A and Account B) on https://ner-project.aixblock.io/.
2.Login into Account A using Container 1 and Account B using Container 2 (e.g., Firefox Multi-Account Containers) to simulate two different sessions.
3.While logged into Account A, go to Account Settings and click “Delete Your Account.”
4.Intercept the DELETE request made to:
DELETE https://ner-project.aixblock.io/api/users/{account_A_id}
5.Modify the user_id in the URL to the user ID of Account B.ccount B.
6.Forward the modified request — the server responds with 201 Created.
7.Attempt to log in to Account B — it fails with “invalid username/password,” confirming the account was deleted.
8.Try registering again using the same email from Account B — registration is successful, further confirming deletion.

Expected behavior
The backend should only allow a user to delete their own account. Proper server-side authorization checks must validate that the user_id in the request matches the currently authenticated user. A user should never be able to delete accounts they do not own.

Impact

Full Unauthorized Deletion: Any user can delete any other user’s account.
Permanent Access & Data Loss: Deleted users are immediately locked out, and re-registration with the same email is possible.
Lack of Safeguards: No confirmation or ownership checks are enforced.
Critical Abuse Potential: Can be automated to mass-delete user accounts, resulting in service disruption and trust loss.
Severe Business Risk: Major violation of user access control, data protection laws, and platform integrity.
