Describe the bug
I have identified an Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR) vulnerability in your user avatar deletion endpoint, which allows an authenticated user to delete the profile picture of any other user by modifying the user ID in the request URL.

To Reproduce
Steps to reproduce the behavior:

1 Create two accounts: Account A and Account B.

2 Log in as Account A and upload a profile picture.

3 Log in as Account B and upload a different profile picture.

4 While authenticated as Account A, send the following DELETE request:

DELETE /api/users/{USER_ID/avatar HTTP/2
Host: ner-project.aixblock.io/.
Replace {user_id} with the ID of Account B.

The avatar of Account B is deleted without any authorization error.

Expected behavior
An authenticated user should only be able to delete their own profile picture. If a user attempts to delete another user's avatar by modifying the user ID in the request, the server should respond with a 403 Forbidden or similar access denied error.

Impact
Any authenticated user can delete the profile picture of any other user by guessing or enumerating user IDs.

Environment
Tested using Chrome Browser and Burp Suite.
